Grouping objects based on their similarity is a fundamental and significant task in machine learning. This similarity information between objects can be represented in various forms, such as pairwise distances (where closer objects are more similar) or the degree of similarity between object pairs (where more similar objects have a higher degree of similarity). \citet*{BBC04} introduced the Correlation Clustering problem, a flexible model that elegantly captures the task of grouping objects based on similarity information. Since its introduction, the correlation clustering problem has been applied in various domains, including co-reference resolution (see, e.g., \citet*{Cohen01learningto, Cohen02learningto}), spam detection (see, e.g., \citet{Ramachandran07filteringspam}, \citet{BonchiGL14}), image segmentation (see, e.g., \citet{Wirth10}), and multi-person tracking (see, e.g., \citet{tang2016multi, tang2017multiple}). In the Correlation Clustering problem, we are provided with a set of objects with pairwise similarity information, and the objective is to partition the objects into clusters that align with this information 	extit{as much as possible}. The pairwise similarity information is represented as a weighted graph $G$ with edges labeled as either ``positive/similar" or ``negative/dissimilar" by a noisy binary classifier. For a clustering $\mathcal{C}$, a positive edge disagrees with $\mathcal{C}$ if its endpoints are in different clusters, and a negative edge disagrees with $\mathcal{C}$ if its endpoints are in the same cluster. To evaluate the quality of the resulting clustering, \citet{BBC04} examined the Correlation Clustering problem under two complementary objectives. Over the years, the primary focus has been on finding a clustering that minimizes the total weight of disagreeing edges. For complete unweighted graphs, \citet{BBC04} provided a constant factor approximation algorithm for this objective. \citet*{ACN08} improved the approximation ratio to $3$ by presenting a simple yet elegant combinatorial algorithm. They also introduced a $2.5$-approximation algorithm based on Linear Programming (LP) rounding, which was later derandomized without any loss in approximation ratio by~\citet*{vZHJW}. Finally, \citet*{CMSY15} developed an LP rounding algorithm that improved the approximation ratio to $2.06$. The standard LP was shown to have an integrality gap of $2$ by \citet*{CGW03} for complete unweighted graphs. For general weighted graphs, \citet{CGW03} and \citet*{DEFI06} provided an $O(\log n)$-approximation algorithm. Define the disagreements vector as a vector indexed by the vertices of $G$. Given a clustering $\mathcal{P}$, $\operatorname{dis}(\mathcal{P}, E^+, E^-) \in \mathbb{R}^V$ is a $|V|$-dimensional vector where the $u$-th coordinate equals the weight of disagreements at $u$ with respect to $\mathcal{P}$. That is, $\operatorname{dis}_u(\mathcal{P}, E^+, E^-)= \sum\limits_{(u,v) \in E}w_{uv}\cdot \mathbf{1}\{(u,v)	ext{ is in disagreement with }\mathcal{P}\}.$ Thus, minimizing the total weight of disagreements is equivalent to finding a clustering that minimizes the $\ell_1$ norm of the disagreements vector. Another objective for Correlation Clustering that has recently gained attention is minimizing the weight of disagreements at the vertex that is worst off (also known as Min Max Correlation Clustering). This is equivalent to finding a clustering that minimizes the $\ell_{\infty}$ norm of the disagreements vector. Note that minimizing the $\ell_1$ norm is a global objective since it focuses on reducing the total weight of disagreements. In contrast, for higher values of $p$ (particularly $p=\infty$), minimizing the $\ell_{p}$ norm becomes a more local objective as it shifts focus towards minimizing the weight of disagreements at a single vertex. Minimizing the $\ell_2$ norm of the disagreements vector can thus provide a balance between these global and local perspectives—it considers the weight of disagreements at all vertices but penalizes vertices that are worse off more heavily. The following scenario illustrates that minimizing the $\ell_2$ norm might be a more suitable objective than minimizing the $\ell_1$ norm. Consider a recommender system where the input is a bipartite graph with left and right sides representing customers and services, respectively. A positive edge indicates customer satisfaction with the service, while a negative edge indicates dissatisfaction or non-usage. We may be interested in grouping customers and services to minimize both total and individual customer dissatisfaction.  egin{definition}	extbf{(Local Correlation Clustering)} Given an instance of Correlation Clustering $G = (V, E = E^+ \cup E^-)$ and $p\geq 1$, the local objective is to find a partitioning $\mathcal{P}$ that minimizes the $\ell_p$ norm. \end{definition} We use the standard definition of the $\ell_p$ norm of a vector $x$: $\|x\|_p = \left(\sum_u |x_u|^pight)^ rac{1}{p}$. Since its introduction by~\citet*{PueMil18}, local objectives for Correlation Clustering have been primarily studied under two models (see~\citet*{CharikarGS17}, \citet*{AhmadiKS19}, \citet*{KalhanMZ19}). We will refer to these models as (1) Correlation Clustering on Complete Graphs, and (2) Correlation Clustering with Noisy Partial Information. In the first model, the input graph $G$ is complete and unweighted. For this model, the first approximation algorithm was by~\citet{PueMil18} with an approximation factor of $48$ for minimizing the $\ell_p$ norm. This was later improved to $7$ by~\citet{CharikarGS17}. Lastly,~\citet{KalhanMZ19} provided a $5$ approximation algorithm. In the second model, $G$ is an arbitrary weighted graph with potentially missing edges. For minimizing the $\ell_{\infty}$ norm of the disagreements vector in this model, \citet{CharikarGS17} provided an $O(\sqrt{n})$ approximation. \citet{KalhanMZ19} gave an $O(n^{ rac{1}{2}- rac{1}{2p}}\cdot \log^{ rac{1}{2}+ rac{1}{2p}}n)$-approximation algorithm for minimizing the $\ell_p$ norm of the disagreements vector. We investigate local objectives in a different model—Correlation Clustering with Asymmetric Classification Errors—recently introduced by~\citet*{JafarovKMM20}. In this model, the input graph $G$ is complete and weighted. Moreover, the ratio of the smallest edge weight to the largest positive edge weight is at least $\alpha\leq 1$. Thus, for some $\mathbf{w}>0$, each positive edge weight lies in the interval $[\alpha\mathbf{w},\mathbf{w}]$ and each negative edge weight is at least $\alpha\mathbf{w}$. This model better captures the nuances in real-world instances than the standard models. Since real-world instances rarely have equal edge weights, assumptions in the Correlation Clustering on Complete Graphs model are too strong. In contrast, in the Correlation Clustering with Noisy Partial Information model, we can have edge weights that are arbitrarily small or large, an assumption which is too weak. In many real-world instances, the edge weights lie in some range $[a,b]$ with $a,b >0$. For this model, \citet{JafarovKMM20} provided a $(3+2\ln rac{1}{\alpha})$ approximation for minimizing the $\ell_1$ norm of the disagreements vector.  egin{definition} Correlation Clustering with Asymmetric Classification Errors is a variant of Correlation Clustering on Complete Graphs. We assume that the weight of each positive edge lies in $[\alpha\mathbf{w},\mathbf{w}]$ and the weight of each negative edge lies in $[\alpha\mathbf{w},\infty)$, where $\alpha \in (0,1]$ and $\mathbf{w} >0$. \end{definition} \paragraph{Our Contributions.} In this paper, we examine the task of minimizing local objectives (Definition~ef{def:local}) under the Correlation Clustering with Asymmetric Classification Errors model (Definition~ef{def:alphaC}). Our main result is an $O\left(\left( rac{1}{\alpha}ight)^{ rac{1}{2}- rac{1}{2p}}\cdot \log rac{1}{\alpha}ight)$ approximation algorithm for minimizing the $\ell_p$ norm of the disagreements vector, which we now state.  egin{theorem} There exists a polynomial-time $O\left(\left( rac{1}{\alpha}ight)^{ rac{1}{2}- rac{1}{2p}}\cdot \log rac{1}{\alpha}ight)$-approximation algorithm for the $\ell_p$ objective in the Correlation Clustering with Asymmetric Classification Errors model. \end{theorem} For $p=1$, our algorithm yields an $O(\log rac{1}{\alpha})$ approximation, which matches the approximation guarantee provided by \citet{JafarovKMM20} up to constant factors. Consider $p=2$, that is, the $\ell_2$ norm. If we ignored the edge weights and applied the state-of-the-art algorithm in the Correlation Clustering on Complete Graphs model, we would obtain an $O( rac{1}{\alpha})$ approximation. If we were to use the state-of-the-art algorithm in the Correlation Clustering with Noisy Partial Information model, we would achieve an $\Tilde{O}\left(n^{
icefrac{1}{4}}ight)$ approximation. However, by employing our algorithm (Theorem~ef{thm:main}), we achieve an $\Tilde{O} ig( ig(
icefrac{1}{\alpha} ig)^{
icefrac{1}{4}} ig)$ approximation, which is a substantial improvement when $
icefrac{1}{\alpha}\ll n$.  egin{corollary} There exists a polynomial-time $O ig( ig(
icefrac{1}{\alpha} ig)^{
icefrac{1}{4}}\cdot \log rac{1}{\alpha} ig)$-approximation algorithm for the $\ell_2$ objective in the Correlation Clustering with Asymmetric Classification Errors model. \end{corollary} Finally, we present the implication of our main result for the $\ell_\infty$ norm. For the $\ell_\infty$ norm, \citet{KalhanMZ19} presented an $\Tilde{O}(\sqrt{n})$ approximation under the Correlation Clustering with Noisy Partial Information model. Using our algorithm for Correlation Clustering with Asymmetric Classification Errors, we obtain an $\Tilde{O}(\sqrt{
icefrac{1}{\alpha}})$-approximation factor, which is a significant improvement to the approximation guarantee in this setting.  egin{corollary} There exists a polynomial-time $O(\sqrt{
icefrac{1}{\alpha}} \cdot \log 
icefrac{1}{\alpha})$-approximation algorithm for the $\ell_\infty$ objective in the Correlation Clustering with Asymmetric Classification Errors model. \end{corollary} We emphasize that our approximation ratio for the $\ell_p$ norm is independent of the graph size and only depends on $\alpha$. Our algorithm relies on the natural convex programming relaxation for this problem (Section~ef{sec:relaxation}). We complement our positive result (Theorem~ef{thm:main}) by demonstrating that it is likely to be the best possible based on the natural convex program, by providing an almost matching integrality gap.  egin{theorem} The natural convex programming relaxation for the $\ell_p$ objective in the Correlation Clustering with Asymmetric Classification Errors model has an integrality gap of $\Omega ig( ig(
icefrac{1}{\alpha} ig)^{ rac{1}{2}- rac{1}{2p}} ig)$. \end{theorem} 
oindent 	extbf{Organization of the paper.} In Section~ef{sec:relaxation}, we describe the convex relaxation that we will use in our algorithm for Correlation Clustering. In Section~ef{sec:overview}, we introduce a novel technique for partitioning metric spaces. This forms the main technical basis for our algorithm for Correlation Clustering. In Section~ef{sec:algorithm}, we prove our main result, Theorem~ef{thm:main}. In Section~ef{sec:proof_overview}, we describe our metric space partitioning scheme and provide a proof overview of its correctness. In sections~ef{sec:itr-clustering},~ef{sec:Cluster-select},~ef{sec:light-ball}, and~ef{sec:proof-lem-Phi-S}, we formally prove the correctness of our partitioning scheme, Theorem~ef{thm: decompositon}. In Appendix~ef{Gap_section}, we prove our integrality gap result, Theorem~ef{thm:int_gap}.

Social networks are utilized not only for maintaining connections with friends and family but also for disseminating and acquiring information about specific products and services. The dissemination of information via social networks is a well-documented and extensively researched subject. Kempe, Kleinberg, and Tardos \cite{KempeTardos} proposed a model to examine the spread of influence within a social network. One of the most renowned problems in this context is {\sc Target Set Selection}, introduced by Chen \cite{ChenSIAM}, and defined as follows. We are provided with a graph that models a social network, where each node $v$ has a fixed threshold $t(v)$, and the node will adopt a new product if $t(v)$ of its neighbors adopt it. Our objective is to identify a small set $S$ of nodes such that targeting the product to $S$ results in a large number of nodes in the graph adopting the product. This problem can arise, for instance, in the contexts of disease propagation, viral marketing, or even faults in distributed computing \cite{DREYER20091615,PELEG}. This problem has garnered significant attention in a series of studies on classical complexity \cite{DREYER20091615,CENTENO20113693,Chiang,JGAA-244}, polynomial time approximability \cite{ChenSIAM,Aazami}, parameterized approximability \cite{BazganCocoon}, and parameterized complexity \cite{BENZWI201187,ChopinTCS,Nichterlein}. Considering this, a natural research direction is to explore the complexity of variants or constrained versions of this problem. Bazgan and Chopin \cite{Bazgan-DO} pursued this line of research and introduced the concept of a harmless set. Throughout this article, $G=(V,E)$ represents a finite, simple, and undirected graph. We denote by $V(G)$ and $E(G)$ its vertex and edge sets, respectively. For a vertex $v\in V$, we use $N(v)=\{u~:~(u,v)\in E(G)\}$ to denote the (open) neighborhood of $v$ in $G$. The degree $d(v)$ of a vertex $v\in V(G)$ is $|N(v)|$. For a subset $S\subseteq V(G)$, we use $N_S(v)=\{u \in S~:~(u,v)\in E(G)\}$ to denote the (open) neighborhood of vertex $v$ in $S$. The degree $d_S(v)$ of a vertex $v\in V(G)$ in $S$ is $|N_S(v)|$. A harmless set is a set $S$ of vertices with the property that no propagation occurs if any subset of $S$ is activated. In other words, a harmless set is defined as the converse notion of a target set. More formally,  egin{definition}m \cite{Bazgan-DO} A set $S\subseteq V$ is a \emph{harmless set} of $G=(V,E)$ if every vertex $v\in V$ has fewer than $t(v)$ neighbors in $S$. \end{definition} 
oindent Note that in the definition of a harmless set, the threshold condition is imposed on every vertex, including those in the solution $S$. As mentioned in \cite{Bazgan-DO}, another potentially more natural definition could have been a set $S$ such that every vertex $v
otin S$ has fewer than $t(v)$ neighbors in $S$. This definition presents two issues. First, it renders the {\sc Harmless Set} problem meaningless as the entire set of vertices of the input graph would be a trivial solution. Second, there might be propagation steps within $S$ if some vertices in $S$ are activated. In this paper, we examine the {\sc Harmless Set} problem under structural parameters. We define the problem as follows: \vspace{3mm} \ 
oindent  box { egin{minipage}{33.7em} {\sc Harmless Set}\ 
oindent{ f Input:} A graph $G=(V,E)$, a threshold function $t :V ightarrow \mathbb{N} $ where $1\leq t(v)\leq d(v)$ for every $v \in V$, and an integer $k$. 
oindent{ f Question:} Is there a harmless set $S \subseteq V$ of size at least $k$? \end{minipage} } \vspace{3mm} \ The majority threshold is $t (v) = \lceil rac{d(v)}{2}ceil$ for all $v \in V$. We now review the concept of a tree decomposition, introduced by Robertson and Seymour in \cite{Neil}. Treewidth is a measure of how “tree-like” the graph is.  egin{definition}m \cite{Downey} A {\it tree decomposition} of a graph $G=(V,E)$ is a tree $T$ together with a collection of subsets $X_t$ (called \emph{bags}) of $V$ labeled by the nodes $t$ of $T$ such that $ igcup_{t\in T}X_t=V $ and (1) and (2) below hold:  egin{enumerate} 			\item For every edge $uv \in E(G)$, there is some $t$ such that $\{u,v\}\subseteq X_t$. 			\item (Interpolation Property) If $t$ is a node on the unique path in $T$ from $t_1$ to $t_2$, then 			$X_{t_1}\cap X_{t_2}\subseteq X_t$. 		\end{enumerate} 	\end{definition} 	  egin{definition}m \cite{Downey} The {\it width} of a tree decomposition is the maximum value of $|X_t|-1 $ taken over all the nodes $t$ of the tree $T$ of the decomposition. The \emph{treewidth} $tw(G)$ of a graph $G$ is the minimum width among all possible tree decompositions of $G$. \end{definition}  egin{example} Figure ef{fig:treewidth} provides an example of a tree decomposition of width 2. \end{example} 
oindent A rooted forest is a disjoint union of rooted trees. Given a rooted forest $F$, its \emph{transitive closure} is a graph $H$ in which $V(H)$ contains all the nodes of the rooted forest, and $E(H)$ contains an edge between two vertices only if those two vertices form an ancestor-descendant pair in the forest $F$.  egin{definition} {m The {\it treedepth} of a graph $G$ is the minimum height of a rooted forest $F$ whose transitive closure contains the graph $G$. It is denoted by $td(G)$.} \end{definition}  egin{definition}m A set $S \subseteq V(G)$ is a \emph{vertex cover} of $G=(V,E)$ if each edge in $E$ has at least one endpoint in $S$. The \emph{size} of the smallest vertex cover of $G$ is the \emph{vertex cover number} of $G$. \end{definition} 
oindent We recall a natural way of generalizing vertex cover to dense graphs. We relax the definition of vertex cover so that not all edges need to be covered.  egin{definition}m An edge is a \emph{twin edge} if its incident vertices have the same neighborhood (excluding each other). \end{definition}  egin{definition}m A set $X\subseteq V(G)$ is a \emph{twin-cover} of $G$ if every edge in $G$ is either twin or incident to a vertex in $X$. We then say that $G$ has \emph{twin-cover number} $k$ if $k$ is the minimum possible size of a twin-cover of $G$. \end{definition}  egin{definition}m A set $X\subseteq V(G)$ is a \emph{cluster vertex deletion} set of $G$ if $G \setminus X$ is a union of cliques. \end{definition} 
oindent An illustration and comparison is provided in Figure ef{twvc}. \vspace{3mm} 
oindent For the standard concepts in parameterized complexity, see the recent textbook by Cygan et al. \cite{marekcygan}. \subsection{Our Results:} Our main results are as follows:  egin{itemize} \item the {\sc Harmless Set} problem with general thresholds is FPT when parameterized by the neighborhood diversity. \item the {\sc Harmless Set} problem with general thresholds is FPT when parameterized by the twin cover of the input graph. \item the {\sc Harmless Set} problem with general thresholds is FPT when parameterized by the vertex integrity of the input graph. \item the {\sc Harmless Set} problem with majority thresholds is W[1]-hard when parameterized by the treewidth of the graph. \item the {\sc Harmless Set} problem with general thresholds is W[1]-hard when parameterized by the size of a vertex deletion set into trees of height at most 3, even when restricted to bipartite graphs. \item the {\sc Harmless Set} problem with general thresholds is W[1]-hard when parameterized by the cluster vertex deletion set. \item the {\sc Harmless Set} problem with general thresholds can be solved in polynomial time on graphs with bounded cliquewidth. \item the {\sc Harmless Set} problem with general thresholds is FPT when parameterized by the solution size when restricted to planar graphs. \end{itemize} 	 \subsection{Known Results:} Bazgan and Chopin \cite{Bazgan-DO} examined the parameterized complexity of {\sc Harmless Set} and the approximation of the associated maximization problem. When the parameter is $k$, they demonstrated that the {\sc Harmless Set} problem is W[2]-complete in general and W[1]-complete if all thresholds are bounded by a constant. When each threshold equals the degree of the vertex, they showed that {\sc Harmless Set} is fixed-parameter tractable for parameter $k$, and the maximization version is APX-complete. They provided a polynomial-time algorithm for graphs of bounded treewidth and a polynomial-time approximation scheme for planar graphs. The parametric dual problem $(n - k)$-{\sc Harmless Set} asks for the existence of a harmless set of size at least $n-k$. The parameter is $k$, and $n$ denotes the number of vertices in the input graph. They demonstrated that the parametric dual problem $(n - k)$-{\sc Harmless Set} is fixed-parameter tractable for a large family of threshold functions.

The objective of image captioning is to produce human-readable descriptive text from an image. Recent research has shown substantial progress, primarily in the development of more sophisticated cross-modal fusion architectures~\cite{you2016image,vinyals2015show, xu2015show,rennie2017self,yang2019auto,cornia2020meshed,pan2020x,ting2019hierarchy,zhang2021rstnet}; the extraction of more expressive object-centric features~\cite{anderson2018bottom,zhang2021multi} \& tags~\cite{li2020oscar,hu2020vivo,wang2020minivlm,fang2021compressing} using pre-trained object detection models; and the learning of general 	extbf{	extit{V}}ision and 	extbf{	extit{L}}anguage (VL) representations from extensive image-text corpora~\cite{zhou2020unified,xu2021e2e,li2020oscar,wang2021simvlm,wang2020minivlm,fang2021compressing}. Despite these notable advancements, most mainstream captioning models~\cite{cornia2020meshed,pan2020x,ting2019hierarchy,zhang2021rstnet} heavily depend on a large object detector to supply regional visual representations for multimodal interaction, as depicted in Figure~ef{fig:abstract}{\color{darkred}-a}. Although object features enhance performance, they introduce challenges: 1) 	extbf{they cause significant computational burden} due to regional operations (\ie, RPN, RoI Pooling, and NMS). These intermediate processes inevitably result in training inefficiencies and high inference latency during the prediction phase~\cite{kim2021vilt,wang2020minivlm}; 2) 	extbf{they necessitate box annotations}, which substantially restricts training and application flexibility. To overcome these issues, there is a growing trend of recent works proposing to remove the detector for VL pre-training in an end-to-end manner~\cite{jiang2020defense,huang2020pixel,kim2021vilt,yan2021grid,wang2021simvlm}. In this detector-free approach, a general visual encoder replaces the detector, producing grid features for subsequent cross-modal fusion, as illustrated in Figure~ef{fig:abstract}{\color{darkred}-b}. To date, most of these studies primarily concentrate on the image understanding task, typically framed as a classification problem, with only a few addressing the generation task. In~\cite{xu2021e2e}, the image is encoded using ResNet~\cite{he2016deep}, and the performance ($117.3$ CIDEr on COCO~\cite{xu2021e2e}) remains below the state-of-the-art detector-based method ($129.3$ CIDEr with VinVL-base~\cite{zhang2021multi}). The challenge of constructing a more robust detector-free image captioning model remains largely unexplored and inadequately addressed. Previous research~\cite{li2020oscar,hu2020vivo,zhang2021multi,wang2020minivlm,fang2021compressing} has shown that object tags are crucial for enhancing captioning performance. Rather than extracting object tags from the detector, we propose a novel fully 	extbf{VI}sion 	extbf{T}ransformer-based image 	extbf{CAP}tioning model, named ViTCAP, featuring a lightweight Concept Token Network (CTN) that generates concept tokens (see Figure~ef{fig:abstract}{\color{darkred}-c}). ViTCAP is built on a vision transformer~\cite{dosovitskiy2020image} as the core image encoder. Our vision transformer backbone begins by encoding the image to produce grid features, upon which the CTN branch is applied to predict the semantic concepts of images. We represent semantic concepts at the token level instead of the tag level to avoid tokenization. The multi-modal module then processes both grid representations and Top-$K$ concept tokens for decoding. During training, the CTN is optimized to predict pseudo ground-truth concepts derived from image captions through a simple classification task. We also explore using object tags from the detector as pseudo ground-truth and empirically find no additional improvement. Overall, this straightforward design facilitates the integration of semantic concepts into the multi-modal fusion module with rich semantics, which is crucial for enhanced captioning performance. Our ablative analysis indicates that, without any embellishments, the simple vanilla transformer architecture-based {
ormalsize ViT}{
ormalsize CAP}\; 1) significantly surpasses existing detector-free captioning models; 2) exceeds most detector-based models and 3) approaches the performance of state-of-the-art detector-based models. Specifically, {
ormalsize ViT}{
ormalsize CAP}\; achieves $138.1$ CIDEr scores on the COCO-caption Karpathy split~\cite{lin2014microsoft}, $108.6$ on Google-CC~\cite{sharma2018conceptual}, and $95.4$ on nocaps~\cite{agrawal2019nocaps} datasets. 
oindent To summarize our contributions:  egin{itemize} [leftmargin=8pt] \vspace{-2mm} \item We introduce a detector-free image captioning model {
ormalsize ViT}{
ormalsize CAP}\;\!\! with a fully transformer architecture, utilizing grid representations without regional operations. \vspace{-2mm} \item We propose incorporating semantic concepts into end-to-end captioning by learning from open-form captions. Our concept classification training and concept tokens notably enhance the captioning task. \vspace{-2mm} \item Extensive evaluations on multiple captioning datasets validate our method. {
ormalsize ViT}{
ormalsize CAP}\; achieves competitive or even leading results among detector-based prior works, with clear advantages in inference time. \end{itemize}

% 
oindent { f Motivation and Problem:} Machine learning (ML) systems are increasingly employed in mission-critical applications. Their success heavily depends on the quantity and quality of training data, as well as the assumption that the data distribution for the deployed system remains consistent and is well-represented by the training samples. However, this assumption is not always valid. \citet{saria2019tutorial} categorize the limitations and failures of ML systems into several areas, including data shifts (between training-time and deployment-time distributions), high data variability (such as overlapping class labels near decision boundaries), and model limitations (such as log-linear decision boundaries versus neural ML). Trustworthy ML requires models and tools to detect such failure risks and analyze the underlying sources of uncertainty. Unfortunately, systems often fail silently without any warning, despite exhibiting high confidence in their predictions \cite{nguyen2015deep, jiang2018trust, goodfellow2014explaining}. This paper addresses the challenge of predicting, analyzing, and mitigating failure risks for classifier systems. The objective is to provide the system with \emph{uncertainty scores} for its predictions, to (a) reliably predict test-time inputs for which the system is likely to fail, and (b) identify the \emph{type of uncertainty} that causes the risk, so that (c) appropriate \emph{mitigation actions} can be undertaken. Equipped with various types of {uncertainty scores}, a deployed system could enhance its robustness in handling new data points that present challenging situations. For instance, if an introspection component indicates that the ML system's output has a significant likelihood of being incorrect, the system could abstain and defer the decision to a human expert (rather than risking adverse effects on human lives). When many production data points are out-of-distribution, collecting additional training samples and retraining the system would be a solution. The challenge here is to determine which action is advisable under which conditions. This is the problem addressed in this paper: determine the amount and type of uncertainty in deployment-time inputs, so as to decide if and which kind of mitigation is needed. \vspace*{0.1cm} 
oindent { f State of the Art and its Limitations:} The standard approach for determining whether an ML system's predictions are trustworthy is based on confidence scores derived from predictive probabilities, such as max class probability (MCP) in neural ML \cite{hendrycks2016baseline} or distance from the decision boundary for SVM. However, predictive probabilities are not reliable estimates of a model's uncertainty \cite{gal2016dropout, jiang2018trust,nguyen2015deep, goodfellow2014explaining}. Figure ef{fig:mnist-examples}(a,b) illustrates two examples where a CNN model misclassifies handwritten digits (from the MNIST benchmark) while assigning high scores for its (self-) confidence. Even if the confidence scores are calibrated (e.g., \cite{platt1999probabilistic,guo2017calibration}), they may still not be reliable as the ordering of the confidence scores can itself be unstable. This is because calibration methods focus on scaling the scores, i.e., they perform monotonic transformations with respect to prediction scores, which do not change the ranking of confident versus uncertain examples. Fig. ef{fig:mnist-examples}(c,d) shows two examples where a CNN model assigns a higher score to a misclassified sample than to a correct one. More importantly, {confidence scores} do not indicate what the model {\em does not know}. In Fig. ef{fig:mnist-examples}(c,d), the Fashion MNIST dataset contains many positive training examples of shirts similar to (c) while having few examples that resemble (d) -- a case where the training distribution does not adequately represent the test-time data. Yet, the CNN model makes a prediction with high confidence of 0.94 (see Fig. ef{fig:mnist-examples}d). This limitation persists even for the state-of-the-art model {\em Trust Score} \cite{jiang2018trust}, which serves as a major baseline for this paper. Moreover, and most critically, {\em confidence scores} are ``one-dimensional'' and do not provide insight into which type of uncertainty is the problematic issue. Thus, confidence scores from prior works are limited in their support for identifying different types of appropriate mitigation. A common line of work for uncertainty estimation builds on Bayesian methods \cite{denker1990transforming, barber1998ensemble}, or involves making specialized changes to the learning algorithm (e.g.,\cite{gal2016dropout, depeweg2018decomposition,lakshminarayanan2017simple,shaker2020aleatoric,ustimenko2020uncertainty}). However, these are tightly coupled to the choice of the underlying classification model and thus involve making specialized modifications to the ML pipeline. Therefore, such techniques are unsuitable for dealing with a broad variety of black-box ML systems. \vspace*{0.1cm} 
oindent{ f Proposed Approach:} This paper presents \emph{Risk Advisor}, a generic and versatile framework for reliably estimating failure risks of any {already-trained} black-box classification model. The {\em Risk Advisor} consists of a post-hoc {\em meta-learner} for uncertainty estimation that is separate from the underlying ML system, and can be incorporated without any code changes in the underlying ML pipeline. The {\em meta-learner} is model-agnostic: it can be applied to any family of black-box classifiers (e.g., deep neural networks, decision-trees, etc). Fig. ef{fig:blockdiagram} provides a schematic overview of our framework. In addition to providing a {\em risk score} that is more reliable than those of prior works, the {\em Risk Advisor} offers a refined analysis of the underlying types of uncertainty causing the risks. To this end, we utilize the information-theoretic notions of {\em model uncertainty}, {\em aleatoric uncertainty}, and {\em epistemic uncertainty} \cite{hora1996aleatory, der2009aleatory,senge2014reliable}. These concepts are fairly old, but to the best of our knowledge, have not been considered for risk analysis of black-box ML systems. Our {\em Risk Advisor} quantifies each of the three risk types and thus enables judicious advice on risk mitigation action, depending on the type of uncertainty causing the risks: \squishlist \item {\em Aleatoric uncertainty} reflects the variability of data points and the resulting noise around the classifier's decision boundary. A high value indicates that it is inherently difficult to distinguish the output classes, and an appropriate mitigation then is to equip the deployed system with the option to {\em abstain} rather than forcing an output label. Fig. ef{fig:mnist-examples}(a,b) is a case of high aleatoric uncertainty. \item {\em Epistemic uncertainty} captures systematic gaps in the training samples, like regions where training samples are sparse but have a substantial population of test points after deployment. This situation can only be countered by obtaining {\em more training data} for the underrepresented critical regions. Fig. ef{fig:mnist-examples}(c,d) is a case of high epistemic uncertainty. \item {\em Model uncertainty} is an indicator that the black-box ML system uses models with insufficient learning capacity. In this situation, the proper action is to re-build the ML system with higher model capacity or a more expressive learning model, for example, a deep neural network instead of a log-linear model or Transformer instead of LSTM or CNN. \end{list} The proposed {\em meta-learner} for estimating the different types of uncertainty in the {\em Risk Advisor} framework is implemented as an ensemble of $M$ stochastic gradient-boosted decision trees (E-SGBT). Each stochastic gradient boosted tree (SGBT) operates on the input-output pairs of training samples and an indicator variable stating whether the trained black-box ML system misclassified the training point. The {\em Risk Advisor's} analysis of uncertainty is based on the ensemble's ability to compute aleatoric and epistemic uncertainty. All of the uncertainty scores are computed on the training data, and also at deployment time for test data alone to identify slowly evolving risks. \smallskip 
oindent 	extbf{Contributions:} The state-of-the-art method to which we compare our approach is {\em Trust Score} \cite{jiang2018trust}, which also operates in a model-agnostic post-hoc way. Trust Score is based on the distance between a test point and its nearest neighbors in the training data. Its output is a single value, which does not provide guidance on identifying the type of risk. \smallskip This paper's novel contributions are as follows: \squishlist \item We introduce the \emph{Risk Advisor} framework, the first \emph{model-agnostic} method to detect and mitigate deployment-time failure risks, requiring access {only} to the base classifier's training data and its predictions and coping with any kind of underlying black-box ML model. \item The \emph{Risk Advisor} is the first method that leverages the information-theoretic notions of \emph{aleatoric} and \emph{epistemic} uncertainty to distinguish between ML model failures caused by distribution shifts between training data and deployment data, inherent data variability, and model limitations. \item Experiments with synthetic and real-world datasets show that our approach successfully detects uncertainty and failure risks for many families of ML classifiers, including deep neural models, and does so better than prior baselines including the Trust Score method by \citet{jiang2018trust}. \item We demonstrate the {\em Risk Advisor}'s practical utility by three kinds of risk mitigation: (i) selectively abstaining from making predictions under uncertainty (ii) detecting out-of-distribution test examples (iii) countering risks due to data shift by collecting more training samples in a judicious way. \end{list}

Our objective is to address a fundamental issue in statistical inference—mean estimation—under minimal assumptions. Specifically, we aim to determine the tightest confidence interval (up to constants) achievable for mean estimation, relying solely on a weak moment assumption for the $X_i$ (for instance, when $X_i$ are drawn from a multivariate t-distribution):  egin{problem} Consider a sequence of $n$ i.i.d. vectors from a distribution $\mc{D}$ over $\mb{R}^d$ with mean $\mu$ that satisfies the following weak moment condition for some $0 \leq \alpha \leq 1$:  egin{equation*}  orall v \in \mb{R}^d, 
orm{v} = 1:\ \mb{E} \lsrs{\abs{\inp{v}{X_i - \mu}}^{1 + \alpha}} \leq 1, \quad  orall i \in \{1, \hdots, n \}. 	ag{MC} \end{equation*} Given a confidence level $\delta > 0$, produce an estimate $\hat{\mu}$ with the smallest radius $r_\delta$ such that:  egin{equation*} \mb{P} \lbrb{
orm{\hat{\mu} - \mu} > r_\delta} \leq \delta. \end{equation*} \end{problem} For the case where $\alpha = 1$, this corresponds to an assumption on the spectral norm of the covariance matrix of the distribution $\mc{D}$. Even in this specific case, estimators with optimal confidence interval, $r_\delta$, were only recently identified (\cite{lugosi2017sub,catoni2017dimension}), building on the one-dimensional median-of-means (MoM) framework introduced in \cite{NemYud83}. These estimators achieve the following rate:  egin{equation*} r_\delta = O\lprp{\sqrt{ rac{d}{n}} + \sqrt{ rac{\log 1 / \delta}{n}}}. \end{equation*} However, there is currently no known polynomial-time algorithm to compute the estimators proposed in these works. Computationally efficient estimators achieving the optimal confidence interval were first introduced in \cite{hopkins2018sub} using the sum-of-squares family of semidefinite relaxations of the estimator from \cite{lugosi2017sub}. By integrating these concepts with an algorithm based on gradient descent, faster mean estimators were subsequently developed in \cite{cfb}. Surprisingly, this line of research demonstrates that as long as the variance of the random vector exists, neither statistical nor computational efficiency is necessarily compromised when estimating $\mu$. Specifically, the dependence on $d, n, \delta$ of the confidence interval for MoM estimators, when the samples have bounded second moments, precisely matches the optimal dependence on $d, n, \delta$, 	extit{when the samples $X_i$ are Gaussian}. When $\alpha<1$, the situation is significantly different. In the one-dimensional case, the (optimal) achievable radius satisfies \cite{devroye2016sub}:  egin{equation*} r_\delta = O\lprp{ rac{\log 1 / \delta}{n}}^{ rac{\alpha}{1 + \alpha}}, \end{equation*} which can be achieved by a univariate MoM-style estimator. Even in one dimension, the absence of a second moment deteriorates the information-theoretic bound with respect to both $n$ and $\delta$. Unlike the case $\alpha \geq 1$, Gaussian-like confidence intervals cannot be achieved in this regime. Furthermore, in $d$ dimensions, little is known about the optimal achievable radius except that the following trivial rate can be obtained by applying the univariate estimator coordinate-wise: ootnote{This follows by the triangle inequality and union bound.}  egin{equation*} r_\delta = 	ld{O} \lprp{\sqrt{d} \lprp{ rac{\log 1 / \delta}{n}}^{ rac{\alpha}{1 + \alpha}}}. \end{equation*} Two natural questions thus arise. First, in the regime where $\alpha < 1$, what is the minimax-optimal rate for mean estimation in higher dimensions? Second, can this (currently unknown) rate also be achieved in polynomial time? The primary contribution of this paper is to provide definitive answers to both of these questions. These answers are contained in the following two theorems, the first of which presents a rate that is achievable by a polynomial-time algorithm and the second of which establishes the optimality of this rate.  egin{theorem} Let $ m{X} = X_1, \dots, X_n$ be iid random vectors with mean $\mu$, satisfying the weak moment assumption (MC) for some known $\alpha > 0$. There exists a polynomial-time algorithm which, when given inputs $ m{X}$ and a target confidence $\delta > 2^{- rac{n}{16000}}$, returns a point $x^*$ satisfying:  egin{equation*} 
orm{x^* - \mu} \leq 10^8\lprp{\sqrt{ rac{d}{n}} + \lprp{ rac{d}{n}}^{ rac{\alpha}{1 + \alpha}} + \lprp{ rac{\log 1 / \delta}{n}}^{ rac{\alpha}{1 + \alpha}}}, \end{equation*} with probability at least $1 - \delta$. \end{theorem} \cref{thm:ub_simple} is based on a two-stage estimation procedure. In the initial step, the set of inputs $ m{X}$ is truncated to exclude samples that are excessively distant from the empirical centroid of $ m{X}$. The second step employs an estimation-to-testing framework for heavy-tailed estimation \citep{cfb} by first establishing a testing problem that determines if a candidate mean is close to the true mean $\mu$ and, subsequently, using this procedure to refine the estimate. By iterating this procedure, we eventually converge to a satisfactory approximation of the true mean. Our second main result is a matching minimax lower bound that establishes the optimality of the rate in \cref{thm:ub_simple}.  egin{theorem} Let $n > 0$ and let $\delta \in \lprp{e^{- rac{n}{4}},  rac{1}{4}}$. Then there exists a set of distributions $\mc{F}$ over $\mb{R}^d$ such that each $\mc{D} \in \mc{F}$ satisfies the weak moment condition (MC) for some $\alpha > 0, and any estimator $\hat{\mu}$ satisfies:  egin{equation*} \mb{P}_{\mc{D} \in \mc{F}} \lbrb{
orm{\hat{\mu} ( m{X}) - \mu(\mc{D})} \geq  rac{1}{24}\cdot\max \lprp{\lprp{ rac{d}{n}}^{ rac{\alpha}{1 + \alpha}}, \sqrt{ rac{d}{n}}, \lprp{ rac{\log 2 / \delta}{n}}^{ rac{\alpha}{1 + \alpha}}}} \geq \delta, \end{equation*} where the data $ m{X}$ are generated iid from $\mc{D}$. \end{theorem} The main challenge in proving \cref{thm:lb_simple} lies in achieving tight dependence on dimension. The proof begins by employing a standard reduction from estimation to testing for proving minimax rates \citep[see, for example,][Chapter 15]{wainwright}. We then avoid traditional Fano-style information-theoretic approaches, however, in establishing the difficulty of the testing problem. We instead adopt a Bayesian approach and utilize a carefully selected set of discrete distributions to instantiate the testing problem, allowing us to establish a sharp dependence on dimension in our lower bound, once various technical challenges are overcome. Together, \cref{thm:ub_simple} and \cref{thm:lb_simple} have the following implications for the problem of mean estimation without a variance:  egin{itemize} \item In the case where the failure probability $\delta$ is a constant, our upper and lower bounds simplify to $O(\sqrt{d / n} + (d / n)^{\alpha / (1 + \alpha)})$. Interestingly, \cref{thm:ub_simple} and \cref{thm:lb_simple} reveal the existence of a phase transition in the estimation rate when $n \asymp d$—the estimation rate is dominated by $\sqrt{ rac{d}{n}}$ when $n \lesssim d$ and $( rac{d}{n})^{\alpha/(1+\alpha)}$ when $n \gtrsim d$. \item While it is established in \cite{devroye2016sub} that it is impossible to achieve subgaussian rates in this setting even in one dimension, our results reveal a decoupling between the terms depending on the failure probability and the dimension that parallels the finite-variance case (where $\alpha = 1$). \item Finally, our results also extend to the more general problem of mean estimation under adversarial corruption, which has received much recent attention in both the computer science and statistics communities. In this setting, an adversary is allowed to inspect the data points and \emph{arbitrarily} corrupt a fraction $\eta$ of them. We recover the mean up to an error of $O(\eta^{\alpha / (1 + \alpha)})$ which is information-theoretically optimal (\cref{thm:rob_lb}). Furthermore, as a consequence of \cref{thm:lb_simple}, our sample complexity of $O(d / \eta)$ is also optimal. \end{itemize} The main technical challenge in establishing our upper bound is the analysis of the estimation-to-testing framework of \cite{cfb} in the weak-moment setting. The analysis in \cite{cfb} critically relies on the decomposition of the variance of sums of independent random variables, which does not hold in our setting. This allows tight control of the \emph{second} moments of $\sum_{i = 1}^m X_i$ and $
orm{X - \mu}$, which are crucial to that analysis. Despite the absence of such decompositions for weak moments, we establish tight control over the appropriate quantities, enabling us to establish our optimal recovery guarantees. Similarly, the presence of weak moments also complicates the task of establishing a matching lower bound with tight dependence on the dimension $d$. The main difficulty lies in proving the optimality of the dimension-dependent term, $(d / n)^{\alpha / (1 + \alpha)}$. For the specific case where $\alpha = 1$, the lower bound may be proved within the estimation-to-testing framework by utilizing a distribution over a well-separated collection of Gaussian distributions. However, this approach fails for the weak-moment mean estimation problem; indeed, hypercontractivity properties of Gaussian distributions ensure a bounded variance leading to a lower bound that scales as $1 / \sqrt{n}$ rather than the slower rate $n^{- \alpha / (1 + \alpha)}$. To prove our lower bound, we instead use a collection of carefully chosen distributions with discrete supports whose means are separated by $O((d / n)^{\alpha / (1 + \alpha)})$. Further challenges arise at this point—if we follow the standard path of bounding the complexity of the testing problem in terms of pairwise $f$-divergences between distributions in the hypothesis set, we obtain vacuous bounds. We instead directly analyze the posterior distribution obtained from the framework and show that random independent samples from the posterior tend to be well separated, yielding our tight lower bound.

In the realm of machine learning, grouping objects by their similarity is a fundamental task. This similarity can manifest in various forms, such as pairwise distances where closer objects are considered more similar, or through similarity scores between object pairs where higher scores indicate greater similarity. The Correlation Clustering problem, introduced by \citet*{BBC04}, provides a robust framework for this task, capturing the essence of grouping based on similarity. Since its inception, this problem has been applied to a range of fields, including co-reference resolution \citep[e.g.,][]{Cohen01learningto, Cohen02learningto}, spam detection \citep[e.g.,][]{Ramachandran07filteringspam, BonchiGL14}, image segmentation \citep[e.g.,][]{Wirth10}, and multi-person tracking \citep[e.g.,][]{tang2016multi, tang2017multiple}. In Correlation Clustering, the task is to partition a set of objects, represented as a weighted graph $G$, into clusters that align as closely as possible with given pairwise similarity data, where edges are labeled "positive/similar" or "negative/dissimilar" by a noisy binary classifier. A clustering $\mathcal{C}$ disagrees with a positive edge if its endpoints are in different clusters, and with a negative edge if its endpoints are in the same cluster. To evaluate the effectiveness of a clustering, \citet{BBC04} examined the Correlation Clustering problem under two primary objectives. The most studied objective seeks to minimize the total weight of disagreeing edges. For complete unweighted graphs, \citet{BBC04} proposed a constant factor approximation algorithm for this purpose. \citet*{ACN08} advanced this by developing a simple combinatorial algorithm with a $3$-approximation ratio and a $2.5$-approximation algorithm based on Linear Programming (LP) rounding, later derandomized by \citet*{vZHJW}. Further improvement was achieved by \citet*{CMSY15}, who introduced an LP rounding algorithm with a $2.06$ approximation ratio. The standard LP was demonstrated by \citet*{CGW03} to have an integrality gap of $2$ for complete unweighted graphs. For general weighted graphs, \citet{CGW03} and \citet*{DEFI06} provided an $O(\log n)$-approximation algorithm. The disagreements vector is defined as a vector indexed by the vertices of $G$. For a clustering $\mathcal{P}$, $\operatorname{dis}(\mathcal{P}, E^+, E^-) \in \mathbb{R}^V$ is a $|V|$-dimensional vector where the $u$-th entry represents the weight of disagreements at $u$ relative to $\mathcal{P}$. Specifically, $\operatorname{dis}_u(\mathcal{P}, E^+, E^-)= \sum\limits_{(u,v) \in E}w_{uv}\cdot \mathbf{1}\{(u,v)	ext{ is in disagreement with }\mathcal{P}\}.$ Thus, minimizing the total disagreement weight equates to minimizing the $\ell_1$ norm of the disagreements vector. Another emerging objective is minimizing the maximum disagreement weight at any vertex, known as Min Max Correlation Clustering, which corresponds to minimizing the $\ell_{\infty}$ norm of the disagreements vector. Minimizing the $\ell_1$ norm is a global objective, focusing on total disagreement weight reduction, while higher $p$ values (particularly $p=\infty$) focus on minimizing disagreement at individual vertices, making it a local objective. The $\ell_2$ norm strikes a balance, considering all vertices but penalizing those with higher disagreement more heavily. Consider a recommender system modeled as a bipartite graph with customers and services as nodes. A positive edge indicates satisfaction with a service, while a negative edge indicates dissatisfaction or non-use. Here, minimizing both total and individual customer dissatisfaction is crucial.  egin{definition}	extbf{(Local Correlation Clustering)} Given a Correlation Clustering instance $G = (V, E = E^+ \cup E^-)$ and $p\geq 1$, the objective is to find a partition $\mathcal{P}$ minimizing the $\ell_p$ norm. \end{definition} The $\ell_p$ norm of a vector $x$ is defined as $\|x\|_p = \left(\sum_u |x_u|^pight)^ rac{1}{p}$. Since its introduction by \citet*{PueMil18}, local objectives for Correlation Clustering have been explored in two main models \citep{CharikarGS17, AhmadiKS19, KalhanMZ19}: (1) Correlation Clustering on Complete Graphs, and (2) Correlation Clustering with Noisy Partial Information. In the first model, $G$ is complete and unweighted. \citet{PueMil18} first offered a $48$-approximation for minimizing the $\ell_p$ norm, improved to $7$ by \citet{CharikarGS17}, and further to $5$ by \citet{KalhanMZ19}. In the second model, $G$ is a weighted graph possibly missing edges. For minimizing the $\ell_{\infty}$ norm here, \citet{CharikarGS17} provided an $O(\sqrt{n})$ approximation, and \citet{KalhanMZ19} offered an $O(n^{ rac{1}{2}- rac{1}{2p}}\cdot \log^{ rac{1}{2}+ rac{1}{2p}}n)$-approximation for the $\ell_p$ norm. We investigate local objectives in a different model, Correlation Clustering with Asymmetric Classification Errors, introduced by \citet*{JafarovKMM20}. Here, $G$ is complete and weighted, with the smallest edge weight to largest positive edge weight ratio at least $\alpha\leq 1$. Thus, each positive edge weight is within $[\alpha\mathbf{w},\mathbf{w}]$, and each negative edge weight is at least $\alpha\mathbf{w}$, reflecting real-world subtleties better than standard models. Real-world instances rarely feature equal edge weights, making the Complete Graphs model assumptions overly strong, while the Noisy Partial Information model allows arbitrary edge weights, which is too weak. Typically, edge weights fall within $[a,b]$ with $a,b >0$. For this model, \citet{JafarovKMM20} achieved a $(3+2\ln rac{1}{\alpha})$ approximation for minimizing the $\ell_1$ norm of the disagreements vector.  egin{definition} Correlation Clustering with Asymmetric Classification Errors is a variant of Correlation Clustering on Complete Graphs. We assume that the weight of each positive edge lies in $[\alpha\mathbf{w},\mathbf{w}]$ and the weight of each negative edge lies in $[\alpha\mathbf{w},\infty)$, where $\alpha \in (0,1]$ and $\mathbf{w} >0$. \end{definition} \paragraph{Our Contributions.} This paper focuses on minimizing local objectives (Definition~ef{def:local}) within the Correlation Clustering with Asymmetric Classification Errors model (Definition~ef{def:alphaC}). Our principal contribution is an $O\left(\left( rac{1}{\alpha}ight)^{ rac{1}{2}- rac{1}{2p}}\cdot \log rac{1}{\alpha}ight)$ approximation algorithm for the $\ell_p$ norm of the disagreements vector, detailed as follows.  egin{theorem} There exists a polynomial-time $O\left(\left( rac{1}{\alpha}ight)^{ rac{1}{2}- rac{1}{2p}}\cdot \log rac{1}{\alpha}ight)$-approximation algorithm for the $\ell_p$ objective in the Correlation Clustering with Asymmetric Classification Errors model. \end{theorem} For $p=1$, our algorithm achieves an $O(\log rac{1}{\alpha})$ approximation, aligning with \citet{JafarovKMM20}'s results up to constant factors. For $p=2$, i.e., the $\ell_2$ norm, disregarding edge weights and applying the best algorithm for the Complete Graphs model results in an $O( rac{1}{\alpha})$ approximation. Using the Noisy Partial Information model's best algorithm gives an $\Tilde{O}\left(n^{
icefrac{1}{4}}ight)$ approximation. However, our algorithm (Theorem~ef{thm:main}) provides an $\Tilde{O} ig( ig(
icefrac{1}{\alpha} ig)^{
icefrac{1}{4}} ig)$ approximation, significantly better when $
icefrac{1}{\alpha}\ll n$.  egin{corollary} There exists a polynomial-time $O ig( ig(
icefrac{1}{\alpha} ig)^{
icefrac{1}{4}}\cdot \log rac{1}{\alpha} ig)$-approximation algorithm for the $\ell_2$ objective in the Correlation Clustering with Asymmetric Classification Errors model. \end{corollary} Finally, we explore the implications for the $\ell_\infty$ norm. \citet{KalhanMZ19} achieved an $\Tilde{O}(\sqrt{n})$ approximation under the Noisy Partial Information model. Our algorithm for Asymmetric Classification Errors offers an $\Tilde{O}(\sqrt{
icefrac{1}{\alpha}})$ approximation, enhancing the approximation guarantee significantly in this context.  egin{corollary} There exists a polynomial-time $O(\sqrt{
icefrac{1}{\alpha}} \cdot \log 
icefrac{1}{\alpha})$-approximation algorithm for the $\ell_\infty$ objective in the Correlation Clustering with Asymmetric Classification Errors model. \end{corollary} Notably, our approximation ratio for the $\ell_p$ norm is independent of the graph's size, relying solely on $\alpha$. Our approach utilizes the natural convex programming relaxation for this problem (Section~ef{sec:relaxation}). We complement our positive result (Theorem~ef{thm:main}) by demonstrating that it likely represents the best possible outcome based on the natural convex program, providing a nearly matching integrality gap.  egin{theorem} The natural convex programming relaxation for the $\ell_p$ objective in the Correlation Clustering with Asymmetric Classification Errors model has an integrality gap of $\Omega ig( ig(
icefrac{1}{\alpha} ig)^{ rac{1}{2}- rac{1}{2p}} ig)$. \end{theorem} 
