Social networks serve dual purposes: maintaining connections with friends and family, and disseminating information about products and services. The dynamics of information spread within these networks has been extensively analyzed and documented. Kempe, Kleinberg, and Tardos \cite{KempeTardos} pioneered a model exploring influence propagation across social networks. Among the significant challenges in this domain is the {\sc Target Set Selection} problem, first introduced by Chen \cite{ChenSIAM}, which is defined as follows: Consider a graph that represents a social network where each node $v$ is assigned a fixed threshold $t(v)$. A node adopts a new product if at least $t(v)$ of its neighbors have adopted it. The objective is to identify a minimal set $S$ of nodes such that initiating the product with $S$ results in widespread adoption throughout the graph. This problem is relevant in contexts such as disease spread, viral marketing, and even fault tolerance in distributed computing systems \cite{DREYER20091615,PELEG}. It has garnered significant attention through various studies focusing on classical complexity \cite{DREYER20091615,CENTENO20113693,Chiang,JGAA-244}, polynomial time approximability \cite{ChenSIAM,Aazami}, parameterized approximability \cite{BazganCocoon}, and parameterized complexity \cite{BENZWI201187,ChopinTCS,Nichterlein}. Given this backdrop, examining the complexity of its variants or constrained versions is a natural research progression. Following this trajectory, Bazgan and Chopin \cite{Bazgan-DO} introduced the concept of a harmless set. Throughout this paper, we denote a finite, simple, and undirected graph by $G=(V,E)$, with $V(G)$ and $E(G)$ representing its vertex and edge sets, respectively. For a vertex $v\in V$, $N(v)=\{u~:~(u,v)\in E(G)\}$ denotes its (open) neighborhood in $G$, and $d(v)=|N(v)|$ represents its degree. For a subset $S\subseteq V(G)$, $N_S(v)=\{u \in S~:~(u,v)\in E(G)\}$ describes the (open) neighborhood of $v$ within $S$, and $d_S(v)=|N_S(v)|$ is its degree within $S$. A harmless set is a set $S$ of vertices such that no propagation occurs if any subset of $S$ is activated. Essentially, a harmless set is the inverse of a target set. More formally:  egin{definition}m \cite{Bazgan-DO} A set $S\subseteq V$ is a \emph{harmless set} of $G=(V,E)$ if every vertex $v\in V$ has fewer than $t(v)$ neighbors in $S$. \end{definition} 
oindent Note that the threshold condition for a harmless set applies to every vertex, including those in $S$. As noted in \cite{Bazgan-DO}, an alternative definition could have been a set $S$ such that each vertex $v
otin S$ has fewer than $t(v)$ neighbors in $S$. However, this definition poses two issues: it renders the {\sc Harmless Set} problem trivial since the entire vertex set would be a solution, and it allows for potential propagation within $S$ if some vertices are activated. This paper investigates the {\sc Harmless Set} problem with respect to structural parameters, defined as follows: \vspace{3mm} \ 
oindent  box { egin{minipage}{33.7em} {\sc Harmless Set}\ 
oindent{ f Input:} A graph $G=(V,E)$, a threshold function $t :V ightarrow \mathbb{N} $ where $1\leq t(v)\leq d(v)$ for every $v \in V$, and an integer $k$. 
oindent{ f Question:} Is there a harmless set $S \subseteq V$ of size at least $k$? \end{minipage} } \vspace{3mm} \ The majority threshold is defined as $t(v) = \lceil rac{d(v)}{2}ceil$ for all $v \in V$. We proceed by revisiting the concept of a tree decomposition, as introduced by Robertson and Seymour in \cite{Neil}. Treewidth is a metric that quantifies how closely a graph resembles a tree.  egin{definition}m \cite{Downey} A {\it tree decomposition} of a graph $G=(V,E)$ consists of a tree $T$ and a collection of subsets $X_t$ (referred to as \emph{bags}) of $V$, labeled by the nodes $t$ of $T$, such that $ igcup_{t\in T}X_t=V $ and the following conditions hold:  egin{enumerate} 			\item For every edge $uv \in E(G)$, there exists some $t$ such that $\{u,v\}\subseteq X_t$. 			\item (Interpolation Property) If $t$ is a node on the unique path in $T$ from $t_1$ to $t_2$, then $X_{t_1}\cap X_{t_2}\subseteq X_t$. 		\end{enumerate} 	\end{definition} 	  egin{definition}m \cite{Downey} The {\it width} of a tree decomposition is the maximum value of $|X_t|-1 $ over all nodes $t$ of the tree $T$. The \emph{treewidth} $tw(G)$ of a graph $G$ is the minimum width among all possible tree decompositions of $G$. \end{definition}  egin{example} Figure ef{fig:treewidth} provides an example of a tree decomposition with width 2. \end{example} 
oindent A rooted forest consists of a collection of disjoint rooted trees. The \emph{transitive closure} of a rooted forest $F$ is a graph $H$ where $V(H)$ includes all nodes of the forest, and $E(H)$ contains an edge between two vertices if they form an ancestor-descendant pair in $F$.  egin{definition} {m The {\it treedepth} of a graph $G$ is the minimum height of a rooted forest $F$ whose transitive closure encompasses $G$. It is denoted by $td(G)$.} \end{definition}  egin{definition}m A set $S \subseteq V(G)$ is a \emph{vertex cover} of $G=(V,E)$ if every edge in $E$ has at least one endpoint in $S$. The \emph{vertex cover number} of $G$ is the size of its smallest vertex cover. \end{definition} 
oindent We recall a natural extension of the vertex cover concept for dense graphs, allowing some edges to remain uncovered.  egin{definition}m An edge is a \emph{twin edge} if its incident vertices share the same neighborhood, excluding each other. \end{definition}  egin{definition}m A set $X\subseteq V(G)$ is a \emph{twin-cover} of $G$ if every edge in $G$ is either a twin or incident to a vertex in $X$. The \emph{twin-cover number} of $G$ is the minimum size of a twin-cover. \end{definition}  egin{definition}m A set $X\subseteq V(G)$ is a \emph{cluster vertex deletion} set of $G$ if $G \setminus X$ is a union of cliques. \end{definition} 
