oindent For foundational concepts in parameterized complexity, refer to the textbook by Cygan et al. \cite{marekcygan}. \subsection{Our Results:} The principal contributions of this work are as follows:  egin{itemize} \item The {\sc Harmless Set} problem with general thresholds is fixed-parameter tractable (FPT) when parameterized by neighborhood diversity. \item It is also FPT when parameterized by the twin cover of the input graph. \item The problem remains FPT when parameterized by the vertex integrity of the input graph. \item With majority thresholds, the {\sc Harmless Set} problem is W[1]-hard when parameterized by the graph's treewidth. \item The problem is W[1]-hard when parameterized by the size of a vertex deletion set that reduces the graph to trees of height at most 3, even when restricted to bipartite graphs. \item It is W[1]-hard when parameterized by the cluster vertex deletion set. \item The problem can be solved in polynomial time for graphs with bounded cliquewidth. \item It is FPT when parameterized by the solution size when restricted to planar graphs. \end{itemize} 	 \subsection{Known Results:} Bazgan and Chopin \cite{Bazgan-DO} explored the parameterized complexity of the {\sc Harmless Set} and the approximation of its maximization variant. They demonstrated that, with parameter $k$, the {\sc Harmless Set} problem is W[2]-complete in general and W[1]-complete if thresholds are bounded by a constant. When each threshold equals the vertex degree, they showed that the problem is fixed-parameter tractable for parameter $k$, and the maximization version is APX-complete. They provided a polynomial-time algorithm for graphs with bounded treewidth and a polynomial-time approximation scheme for planar graphs. The parametric dual problem $(n - k)$-{\sc Harmless Set} seeks a harmless set of size at least $n-k$, where $k$ is the parameter and $n$ is the number of vertices in the graph. They proved that this dual problem is fixed-parameter tractable for a broad class of threshold functions.

Image captioning is a task dedicated to generating descriptive text from images. Significant progress has been made recently, primarily due to advancements in cross-modal fusion architectures~\cite{you2016image,vinyals2015show,xu2015show,rennie2017self,yang2019auto,cornia2020meshed,pan2020x,ting2019hierarchy,zhang2021rstnet}, the development of more expressive object-centric features~\cite{anderson2018bottom,zhang2021multi}, and the use of tags~\cite{li2020oscar,hu2020vivo,wang2020minivlm,fang2021compressing} derived from pre-trained object detection models. Additionally, efforts to learn general 	extbf{	extit{V}}ision and 	extbf{	extit{L}}anguage (VL) representations from extensive image-text corpora have been noteworthy~\cite{zhou2020unified,xu2021e2e,li2020oscar,wang2021simvlm,wang2020minivlm,fang2021compressing}. Despite these advancements, most prevalent captioning models~\cite{cornia2020meshed,pan2020x,ting2019hierarchy,zhang2021rstnet} continue to depend on cumbersome object detectors to provide regional visual representations for multimodal interaction, as illustrated in Figure~ef{fig:abstract}{\color{darkred}-a}. While object features enhance performance, they introduce several challenges: 1) 	extbf{They impose a heavy computational burden} due to regional operations such as RPN, RoI Pooling, and NMS, leading to inefficiencies in training and high latency during inference~\cite{kim2021vilt,wang2020minivlm}; 2) 	extbf{They necessitate box annotations}, which restricts the flexibility of training and application. To overcome these issues, recent works are shifting towards eliminating the detector in VL pre-training, adopting an end-to-end approach~\cite{jiang2020defense,huang2020pixel,kim2021vilt,yan2021grid,wang2021simvlm}. In this detector-free framework, a general visual encoder replaces the detector, generating grid features for subsequent cross-modal fusion, as depicted in Figure~ef{fig:abstract}{\color{darkred}-b}. To date, most of these approaches focus on image understanding tasks, usually framed as classification problems, with few addressing generation tasks. In~\cite{xu2021e2e}, images are encoded using ResNet~\cite{he2016deep}, achieving a performance of $117.3$ CIDEr on COCO~\cite{xu2021e2e}, which remains below the state-of-the-art detector-based method ($129.3$ CIDEr with VinVL-base~\cite{zhang2021multi}). The challenge of developing a robust detector-free image captioning model remains largely unexplored. Previous research~\cite{li2020oscar,hu2020vivo,zhang2021multi,wang2020minivlm,fang2021compressing} has highlighted the crucial role of object tags in enhancing captioning performance. Instead of extracting object tags from detectors, we introduce a novel image captioning model based entirely on a 	extbf{VI}sion 	extbf{T}ransformer, named ViTCAP, which incorporates a lightweight Concept Token Network (CTN) to generate concept tokens (see Figure~ef{fig:abstract}{\color{darkred}-c}). ViTCAP is built upon a vision transformer~\cite{dosovitskiy2020image} as the primary image encoder. The vision transformer backbone encodes the image to produce grid features, upon which the CTN branch predicts semantic image concepts. These semantic concepts are represented at the token level, rather than the tag level, to avoid tokenization. The multimodal module then utilizes both grid representations and Top-$K$ concept tokens for decoding. During training, the CTN is optimized to predict pseudo ground-truth concepts extracted from image captions via a straightforward classification task. We also explored using object tags from detectors as pseudo ground-truth and found no additional benefits. This straightforward design facilitates the integration of rich semantic concepts into the multimodal fusion module, which is crucial for improved captioning performance. Our ablation studies reveal that, without any complex modifications, the simple vanilla transformer architecture of {
ormalsize ViT}{
ormalsize CAP}\; 1) significantly outperforms existing detector-free captioning models; 2) surpasses most detector-based models; and 3) approaches the state-of-the-art detector-based models. Specifically, {
ormalsize ViT}{
ormalsize CAP}\; achieves $138.1$ CIDEr on the COCO-caption Karpathy split~\cite{lin2014microsoft}, $108.6$ on Google-CC~\cite{sharma2018conceptual}, and $95.4$ on nocaps~\cite{agrawal2019nocaps} datasets. 
oindent In summary, our contributions are as follows:  egin{itemize} [leftmargin=8pt] \vspace{-2mm} \item We introduce a detector-free image captioning model, {
ormalsize ViT}{
ormalsize CAP}, utilizing a fully transformer-based architecture that leverages grid representations without regional operations. \vspace{-2mm} \item We propose incorporating semantic concepts into end-to-end captioning by learning from open-form captions. Our approach to concept classification training and the use of concept tokens significantly enhances captioning performance. \vspace{-2mm} \item Comprehensive evaluations across multiple captioning datasets validate our method's effectiveness. {
ormalsize ViT}{
ormalsize CAP}\; achieves competitive or superior results compared to detector-based models, with notable advantages in inference time. \end{itemize}

