oindent { f Motivation and Problem:} Machine learning (ML) systems have become integral to mission-critical applications, relying heavily on the quality and quantity of their training data. Their effectiveness assumes that the data distribution at deployment mirrors that of the training phase, which is often not the case. \citet{saria2019tutorial} identify several limitations and failures in ML systems, including distribution shifts between training and deployment, high data variability, and model inadequacies. To ensure trustworthy ML, it is crucial to develop models and tools capable of detecting potential failure risks and understanding the sources of uncertainty. Unfortunately, many systems fail without warning, maintaining high confidence in their predictions despite underlying issues \cite{nguyen2015deep, jiang2018trust, goodfellow2014explaining}. This paper tackles the challenge of predicting, analyzing, and mitigating failure risks in classifier systems. We aim to generate \emph{uncertainty scores} for predictions to (a) predict inputs likely to cause system failure, (b) identify the \emph{type of uncertainty} contributing to the risk, and (c) implement suitable \emph{mitigation actions}. By leveraging various \emph{uncertainty scores}, a system can enhance its robustness in handling challenging new data points. For example, if an introspection component suggests a high probability of error in the ML system's output, the system could defer the decision to a human expert to avoid negative consequences. When faced with numerous out-of-distribution data points, gathering additional training samples and retraining the system could be a solution. The key challenge is determining the appropriate action for specific conditions, which this paper addresses by assessing the level and type of uncertainty in deployment-time inputs to decide on necessary mitigation measures. \vspace*{0.1cm} 
oindent { f State of the Art and its Limitations:} The conventional method for assessing the trustworthiness of ML predictions relies on confidence scores derived from predictive probabilities, such as max class probability (MCP) in neural ML \cite{hendrycks2016baseline} or proximity to the decision boundary in SVMs. However, these probabilities often fail to accurately represent a model's uncertainty \cite{gal2016dropout, jiang2018trust,nguyen2015deep, goodfellow2014explaining}. Figure ef{fig:mnist-examples}(a,b) illustrates instances where a CNN model misclassifies MNIST digits while maintaining high confidence scores. Even calibrated confidence scores, designed to scale scores without altering their ranking \cite{platt1999probabilistic,guo2017calibration}, may still be untrustworthy. Figure ef{fig:mnist-examples}(c,d) demonstrates cases where a CNN model assigns a higher score to an incorrect sample than a correct one. More critically, {confidence scores} do not convey what the model {\em does not know}. In Fig. ef{fig:mnist-examples}(c,d), the Fashion MNIST dataset contains many positive training examples similar to (c) but few resembling (d), indicating a mismatch between training and test data distributions. Despite this, the CNN model predicts with a high confidence of 0.94 (see Fig. ef{fig:mnist-examples}d). This limitation persists even in advanced models like {\em Trust Score} \cite{jiang2018trust}, which serves as a key baseline for this work. Furthermore, {\em confidence scores} are inherently ``one-dimensional'' and fail to provide insights into the specific types of uncertainty causing issues. Thus, confidence scores from previous works are limited in their capacity to identify suitable mitigation strategies. A prevalent approach to estimating uncertainty involves Bayesian methods \cite{denker1990transforming, barber1998ensemble} or altering the learning algorithm \cite{gal2016dropout, depeweg2018decomposition,lakshminarayanan2017simple,shaker2020aleatoric,ustimenko2020uncertainty}. However, these methods are often tightly linked to the underlying classification model, necessitating specialized modifications to the ML pipeline. Consequently, they are not suited for a wide range of black-box ML systems. \vspace*{0.1cm} 
oindent{ f Proposed Approach:} This paper introduces \emph{Risk Advisor}, a flexible framework designed to reliably estimate failure risks for any {already-trained} black-box classification model. The {\em Risk Advisor} employs a post-hoc {\em meta-learner} for uncertainty estimation, independent of the underlying ML system, requiring no changes to the existing ML pipeline. The {\em meta-learner} is model-agnostic and applicable to various black-box classifiers, such as deep neural networks or decision trees. Fig. ef{fig:blockdiagram} provides a schematic overview of our framework. Beyond offering a more dependable {\em risk score} than previous methods, the {\em Risk Advisor} delivers a nuanced analysis of the types of uncertainty contributing to risks. We leverage information-theoretic concepts of {\em model uncertainty}, {\em aleatoric uncertainty}, and {\em epistemic uncertainty} \cite{hora1996aleatory, der2009aleatory,senge2014reliable}. While these concepts are well-established, they have not been applied to risk analysis of black-box ML systems. Our {\em Risk Advisor} quantifies each uncertainty type, enabling informed decisions on risk mitigation actions based on the uncertainty source: \squishlist \item {\em Aleatoric uncertainty} indicates data variability and noise near the classifier's decision boundary. High values suggest inherent difficulty in distinguishing output classes, and the system should consider {\em abstaining} rather than forcing a label. Fig. ef{fig:mnist-examples}(a,b) exemplifies high aleatoric uncertainty. \item {\em Epistemic uncertainty} points to gaps in training samples, where sparse training data contrasts with a significant test point population post-deployment. This requires acquiring {\em more training data} for underrepresented regions. Fig. ef{fig:mnist-examples}(c,d) illustrates high epistemic uncertainty. \item {\em Model uncertainty} signals that the ML system employs models with insufficient learning capacity. The solution is to upgrade the ML system with higher capacity models or more expressive learning models, such as transitioning from a log-linear model to a deep neural network or from LSTM to Transformer. \end{list} The {\em meta-learner} in the {\em Risk Advisor} framework estimates uncertainty types using an ensemble of $M$ stochastic gradient-boosted decision trees (E-SGBT). Each SGBT processes input-output pairs of training samples and an indicator of whether the black-box ML system misclassified the sample. The {\em Risk Advisor's} uncertainty analysis is based on the ensemble's capability to compute aleatoric and epistemic uncertainty. These scores are calculated on training data and at deployment for test data to identify evolving risks. \smallskip 
oindent 	extbf{Contributions:} Our approach is compared to the state-of-the-art method {\em Trust Score} \cite{jiang2018trust}, which also functions post-hoc and model-agnostic. Trust Score relies on the distance between a test point and its nearest training data neighbors, yielding a single value without guidance on risk type identification. \smallskip This paper's contributions include: \squishlist \item Introduction of the \emph{Risk Advisor} framework, the first \emph{model-agnostic} method for detecting and mitigating deployment-time failure risks, requiring only access to the base classifier's training data and predictions, adaptable to any black-box ML model. \item Novel use of information-theoretic notions of \emph{aleatoric} and \emph{epistemic} uncertainty to differentiate ML model failures due to distribution shifts, inherent data variability, and model limitations. \item Empirical validation with synthetic and real-world datasets, demonstrating our approach's superior ability to detect uncertainty and failure risks across various ML classifiers, including deep neural models, compared to baselines like Trust Score \cite{jiang2018trust}. \item Practical utility of the {\em Risk Advisor} in three risk mitigation strategies: (i) selectively abstaining from uncertain predictions, (ii) identifying out-of-distribution test examples, and (iii) addressing data shift risks by strategically collecting more training samples. \end{list}

This paper addresses a core challenge in statistical inference: the estimation of the mean, under minimal assumptions. Specifically, we aim to determine the most precise confidence interval (up to constant factors) for mean estimation, given only a weak moment assumption on the $X_i$. For instance, consider $X_i$ drawn from a multivariate t-distribution:  egin{problem} Suppose we have a sequence of $n$ i.i.d. vectors derived from a distribution $\mc{D}$ over $\mb{R}^d$ with a mean $\mu$, satisfying a weak moment condition for some $0 \leq \alpha \leq 1$:  egin{equation*}  orall v \in \mb{R}^d, 
orm{v} = 1:\ \mb{E} \lsrs{\abs{\inp{v}{X_i - \mu}}^{1 + \alpha}} \leq 1, \quad  orall i \in \{1, \hdots, n \}. 	ag{MC} \end{equation*} For a given confidence level $\delta > 0$, produce an estimate $\hat{\mu}$ with the minimal radius $r_\delta$ such that:  egin{equation*} \mb{P} \lbrb{
orm{\hat{\mu} - \mu} > r_\delta} \leq \delta. \end{equation*} \end{problem} When $\alpha = 1$, this corresponds to an assumption regarding the spectral norm of the covariance matrix of $\mc{D}$. Even in this scenario, optimal estimators for the confidence interval $r_\delta$ have only recently been discovered (\cite{lugosi2017sub,catoni2017dimension}), leveraging the one-dimensional median-of-means (MoM) framework from \cite{NemYud83}. These estimators achieve:  egin{equation*} r_\delta = O\lprp{\sqrt{ rac{d}{n}} + \sqrt{ rac{\log 1 / \delta}{n}}}. \end{equation*} However, no known polynomial-time algorithm can compute these estimators. Computationally efficient estimators achieving the optimal confidence interval were first introduced in \cite{hopkins2018sub}, utilizing the sum-of-squares family of semidefinite relaxations of the estimator from \cite{lugosi2017sub}. By integrating these ideas with a gradient descent-based algorithm, faster mean estimators were later developed in \cite{cfb}. Remarkably, this research indicates that, provided the variance of the random vector exists, neither statistical nor computational efficiency is compromised in estimating $\mu$. Specifically, the dependence of the confidence interval for MoM estimators on $d, n, \delta$, when samples have bounded second moments, aligns exactly with the optimal dependence when the samples $X_i$ are Gaussian. For $\alpha<1$, the situation changes significantly. In the one-dimensional scenario, the (optimal) achievable radius is given by \cite{devroye2016sub}:  egin{equation*} r_\delta = O\lprp{ rac{\log 1 / \delta}{n}}^{ rac{\alpha}{1 + \alpha}}, \end{equation*} achievable by a univariate MoM-style estimator. In one dimension, lacking a second moment worsens the information-theoretic bound concerning both $n$ and $\delta$. Unlike the case $\alpha \geq 1$, Gaussian-like confidence intervals are unattainable in this regime. Furthermore, in $d$ dimensions, little is known about the optimal achievable radius except for the following trivial rate obtained by applying the univariate estimator coordinate-wise: ootnote{This follows by the triangle inequality and union bound.}  egin{equation*} r_\delta = 	ld{O} \lprp{\sqrt{d} \lprp{ rac{\log 1 / \delta}{n}}^{ rac{\alpha}{1 + \alpha}}}. \end{equation*} This raises two natural questions. First, what is the minimax-optimal rate for mean estimation in higher dimensions when $\alpha < 1$? Second, can this (currently unknown) rate be achieved in polynomial time? The main contribution of this paper is to provide definitive answers to both questions. These answers are encapsulated in the following theorems, where the first theorem presents a rate achievable by a polynomial-time algorithm, and the second confirms the optimality of this rate.  egin{theorem} Let $ m{X} = X_1, \dots, X_n$ be iid random vectors with mean $\mu$, satisfying the weak moment assumption (MC) for some known $\alpha > 0$. There exists a polynomial-time algorithm which, given inputs $ m{X}$ and a target confidence $\delta > 2^{- rac{n}{16000}}$, returns a point $x^*$ such that:  egin{equation*} 
orm{x^* - \mu} \leq 10^8\lprp{\sqrt{ rac{d}{n}} + \lprp{ rac{d}{n}}^{ rac{\alpha}{1 + \alpha}} + \lprp{ rac{\log 1 / \delta}{n}}^{ rac{\alpha}{1 + \alpha}}}, \end{equation*} with probability at least $1 - \delta$. \end{theorem} \cref{thm:ub_simple} is based on a two-stage estimation process. Initially, the input set $ m{X}$ is truncated to remove samples too distant from the empirical centroid of $ m{X}$. The second stage employs an estimation-to-testing framework for heavy-tailed estimation \citep{cfb}, starting with a testing problem to determine if a candidate mean is close to the true mean $\mu$, and then using this to refine the estimate. By iterating this procedure, we converge to a satisfactory approximation of the true mean. Our second major result is a matching minimax lower bound, confirming the optimality of the rate in \cref{thm:ub_simple}.  egin{theorem} Let $n > 0$ and let $\delta \in \lprp{e^{- rac{n}{4}},  rac{1}{4}}$. Then there exists a set of distributions $\mc{F}$ over $\mb{R}^d$ such that each $\mc{D} \in \mc{F}$ adheres to the weak moment condition (MC) for some $\alpha > 0$, and any estimator $\hat{\mu}$ satisfies:  egin{equation*} \mb{P}_{\mc{D} \in \mc{F}} \lbrb{
orm{\hat{\mu} ( m{X}) - \mu(\mc{D})} \geq  rac{1}{24}\cdot\max \lprp{\lprp{ rac{d}{n}}^{ rac{\alpha}{1 + \alpha}}, \sqrt{ rac{d}{n}}, \lprp{ rac{\log 2 / \delta}{n}}^{ rac{\alpha}{1 + \alpha}}}} \geq \delta, \end{equation*} where the data $ m{X}$ are generated iid from $\mc{D}$. \end{theorem} The primary challenge in proving \cref{thm:lb_simple} is achieving tight dependence on dimension. The proof starts with a standard reduction from estimation to testing to establish minimax rates \citep[see, for example,][Chapter 15]{wainwright}. We diverge from conventional Fano-style information-theoretic methods in establishing the difficulty of the testing problem. Instead, we adopt a Bayesian approach, using a carefully chosen set of discrete distributions to define the testing problem, which allows us to achieve a sharp dependence on dimension in our lower bound after overcoming several technical obstacles. Together, \cref{thm:ub_simple} and \cref{thm:lb_simple} have significant implications for mean estimation without variance:  egin{itemize} \item When the failure probability $\delta$ is constant, our bounds simplify to $O(\sqrt{d / n} + (d / n)^{\alpha / (1 + \alpha)})$. Notably, \cref{thm:ub_simple} and \cref{thm:lb_simple} indicate a phase transition in the estimation rate when $n \asymp d$—the estimation rate is dominated by $\sqrt{ rac{d}{n}}$ when $n \lesssim d$ and $( rac{d}{n})^{\alpha/(1+\alpha)}$ when $n \gtrsim d$. \item Although \cite{devroye2016sub} established that subgaussian rates are unattainable in this setting even in one dimension, our results demonstrate a separation between the terms dependent on failure probability and dimension, paralleling the finite-variance scenario (where $\alpha = 1$). \item Lastly, our results extend to the broader problem of mean estimation under adversarial corruption, a topic of recent interest in both computer science and statistics. In this context, an adversary can inspect and \emph{arbitrarily} corrupt a fraction $\eta$ of data points. We recover the mean with an error of $O(\eta^{\alpha / (1 + \alpha)})$, which is information-theoretically optimal (\cref{thm:rob_lb}). Furthermore, as a consequence of \cref{thm:lb_simple}, our sample complexity of $O(d / \eta)$ is also optimal. \end{itemize} The key technical challenge in establishing our upper bound involves analyzing the estimation-to-testing framework of \cite{cfb} in the weak-moment context. The analysis in \cite{cfb} relies heavily on the decomposition of variance of sums of independent random variables, which does not hold in our setting. This decomposition is crucial for controlling the \emph{second} moments of $\sum_{i = 1}^m X_i$ and $
orm{X - \mu}$. Despite the absence of such decompositions for weak moments, we manage to tightly control the relevant quantities, enabling us to achieve our optimal recovery guarantees. Similarly, the weak moment condition complicates establishing a matching lower bound with precise dimension dependence. The main hurdle is proving the optimality of the dimension-dependent term, $(d / n)^{\alpha / (1 + \alpha)}$. For the specific case of $\alpha = 1$, the lower bound can be proven within the estimation-to-testing framework by employing a distribution over a well-separated collection of Gaussian distributions. However, this approach fails for weak-moment mean estimation; the hypercontractivity properties of Gaussian distributions ensure a bounded variance, resulting in a lower bound scaling as $1 / \sqrt{n}$ instead of the slower rate $n^{- \alpha / (1 + \alpha)}$. To establish our lower bound, we use a collection of strategically chosen distributions with discrete supports whose means are separated by $O((d / n)^{\alpha / (1 + \alpha)})$. Further challenges arise here—if we follow the standard approach of bounding the complexity of the testing problem using pairwise $f$-divergences between distributions in the hypothesis set, we obtain trivial bounds. Instead, we directly analyze the posterior distribution from the framework and demonstrate that random independent samples from the posterior tend to be well separated, yielding our tight lower bound.

oindent Logo is one of the core components of brand image, and identifying logos can statistically analyze the exposure and influence of a brand from an amount number of media sources, including social media, television broadcasts, and print advertisements. The significance of accurate logo recognition extends beyond mere brand analysis; it plays a crucial role in intellectual property protection, competitive analysis, and targeted advertising. The process of logo recognition has evolved significantly with advancements in computer vision and machine learning. Traditional methods relied heavily on handcrafted features and domain-specific knowledge, which often limited their adaptability and performance across diverse datasets \cite{liu2017deep}. In contrast, modern approaches leverage deep learning architectures, enabling more generalized and scalable solutions. \subsection{Motivation and Scope} The primary motivation for advancing logo recognition technologies stems from the increasing volume of visual data generated in digital and physical media. As brands invest heavily in visual advertising, the need for automated systems that can accurately and efficiently analyze this data has become paramount. Furthermore, with the proliferation of counterfeit products, robust logo recognition systems are vital for enforcing trademark rights and ensuring brand authenticity \cite{su2017deep}. This paper focuses on exploring the latest methodologies in deep learning for logo recognition, including the application of CNNs and transfer learning strategies. We review state-of-the-art models and discuss their strengths and limitations in various contexts. Additionally, we highlight the challenges that remain in this field and propose potential directions for future research. \subsection{Contributions} Our contributions are threefold:  egin{enumerate} \item We conduct a comprehensive review of existing deep learning models for logo recognition, comparing their architectures, datasets, and performance metrics. \item We introduce a novel hybrid model that combines CNNs with attention mechanisms to enhance recognition accuracy by focusing on critical logo elements. \item We provide an extensive evaluation of the proposed model on multiple benchmark datasets, demonstrating its superiority in terms of precision and recall compared to existing approaches. \end{enumerate} 
oindent In recent years, the landscape of logo recognition has been transformed by deep learning techniques. Early methods, such as those based on Scale-Invariant Feature Transform (SIFT) and Histogram of Oriented Gradients (HOG), laid the groundwork but were often limited by their reliance on feature engineering \cite{lowe2004distinctive}. These methods struggled with variations in scale, rotation, and occlusion, which are commonplace in real-world scenarios. The advent of deep learning, particularly CNNs, marked a paradigm shift. CNNs automatically learn hierarchical feature representations, making them well-suited for complex image classification tasks. Krizhevsky et al.'s pioneering work on AlexNet \cite{krizhevsky2012imagenet} demonstrated the potential of deep learning in visual recognition, sparking interest in its application to logos. Several studies have since focused on adapting CNNs for logo recognition. For example, Bianco et al. \cite{bianco2015logo} proposed a deep learning framework specifically tailored for recognizing logos in natural scenes. Their approach utilized a combination of pre-trained networks and fine-tuning techniques to improve accuracy. Transfer learning has emerged as a powerful tool in this domain, allowing models pre-trained on large datasets to be adapted for specific tasks with limited data. This technique has proven effective in logo recognition, as demonstrated by Su et al. \cite{su2017deep}, who fine-tuned VGGNet for logo classification tasks, achieving significant improvements over traditional methods. More recently, the integration of attention mechanisms in deep learning models has shown promise. Attention mechanisms enable models to focus on relevant parts of an image, which is particularly useful for logos that may contain intricate details \cite{zhang2020attention}. 
oindent Our proposed method builds upon the strengths of CNNs and introduces an attention-based hybrid model designed to enhance the recognition of logos with complex structures. The key components of our approach are outlined below: \subsection{Model Architecture} The architecture of our model consists of a base CNN for feature extraction, followed by an attention module that selectively emphasizes informative regions of the logo. The final classification is performed using a fully connected layer with a softmax activation function.  egin{itemize} \item 	extbf{Base CNN:} We utilize ResNet-50 \cite{he2016deep} as the backbone for feature extraction, owing to its proven capability in capturing deep hierarchical features. \item 	extbf{Attention Module:} Inspired by the work of Vaswani et al. \cite{vaswani2017attention}, our attention mechanism computes a weighted sum of feature maps, allowing the model to focus on salient parts of the logo. \item 	extbf{Output Layer:} The attention-enhanced features are fed into a fully connected layer, which produces a probability distribution over the possible logo classes. \end{itemize} \subsection{Training and Optimization} The training process involves fine-tuning the pre-trained ResNet-50 on our logo-specific dataset. We employ data augmentation techniques, including random rotation, scaling, and color jittering, to increase the robustness of the model. The optimization is performed using the Adam optimizer \cite{kingma2014adam}, with a learning rate schedule that reduces the learning rate upon plateauing validation loss. 
oindent To evaluate the performance of our proposed model, we conducted experiments on several widely-used logo recognition datasets, including Logos-32plus \cite{su2017deep} and FlickrLogos-32 \cite{romberg2011scalable}. \subsection{Performance Metrics} We assessed the model's performance using standard metrics such as accuracy, precision, recall, and F1-score. These metrics provide a comprehensive view of the model's effectiveness in both recognizing and distinguishing between different logo classes. \subsection{Comparative Analysis} Our experimental results indicate that the proposed hybrid model outperforms existing state-of-the-art approaches in terms of both accuracy and recall. The inclusion of the attention mechanism was particularly beneficial in scenarios where logos were partially occluded or embedded within complex backgrounds.  egin{table}[h] \centering \caption{Performance Comparison on the FlickrLogos-32 Dataset}  egin{tabular}{lcccc} \hline 	extbf{Model} & 	extbf{Accuracy (\%)} & 	extbf{Precision (\%)} & 	extbf{Recall (\%)} & 	extbf{F1-score (\%)} \ \hline Bianco et al. \cite{bianco2015logo} & 85.0 & 86.2 & 84.5 & 85.3 \ Su et al. \cite{su2017deep} & 88.5 & 89.0 & 87.8 & 88.4 \ Proposed Model & 	extbf{91.7} & 	extbf{92.3} & 	extbf{91.0} & 	extbf{91.6} \ \hline \end{tabular} \end{table} 
