From G. Hinton's Turing-award-winning work on 	extit{backpropagation} in 1986 \cite{Rumelhart1986LearningRB} to modern optimizers standardized in popular deep learning libraries like Google's Tensorflow \cite{Abadi2016TensorFlowAS} and Facebook's PyTorch\cite{NEURIPS2019_9015}, the 	extit{gradient} descent algorithm has been the cornerstone of training deep neural networks. Despite the algorithm's pivotal role in facilitating breakthroughs across various AI domains, its inherent vulnerability to privacy attacks, such as data reconstruction, has raised significant concerns regarding the confidentiality of sensitive training data. As neural networks increasingly permeate sectors requiring stringent data protection, such as healthcare and finance, the implications of these privacy risks warrant a comprehensive investigation. Federated learning, a paradigm designed to address privacy issues by decentralizing model training, paradoxically introduces new avenues for gradient-based attacks. In federated learning, participants collaboratively train a global model by sharing model updates instead of raw data. Although this approach ostensibly enhances privacy by keeping data local, the gradients transmitted during the training process can inadvertently encode substantial information about the underlying data distribution. This encoding forms the basis of the aforementioned data reconstruction attack, where adversaries exploit these gradients to reverse-engineer the input data. The susceptibility of gradient-based methods to privacy attacks has catalyzed a burgeoning field of research focused on mitigating these risks. Early work by Phong et al. \cite{Phong2018} explored cryptographic techniques, such as secure multiparty computation and homomorphic encryption, to safeguard gradients. These methods, while effective in theory, often entail significant computational overhead, impeding their practicality in real-time applications. Another promising line of defense involves differential privacy (DP), which introduces noise to the gradients, thereby obfuscating the data-specific information they carry \cite{Abadi2016DeepLW}. Although DP provides a rigorous privacy guarantee, its integration into gradient descent can degrade model performance, creating a trade-off between accuracy and privacy preservation. Recent advancements in privacy-preserving machine learning have focused on developing more efficient algorithms that balance this trade-off. For instance, the work by Papernot et al. \cite{Papernot2016SemiSupervisedKD} leverages a teacher-student framework to distill knowledge from an ensemble of models trained on different subsets of data, thus reducing the amount of information leakage from any single model. Similarly, methods such as SplitNN \cite{Gupta2018DistributedLD} distribute the model architecture itself, ensuring that sensitive data never leaves the local environment. Despite these advancements, the evolving landscape of privacy attacks necessitates continuous innovation. Sophisticated attack vectors, particularly those employing generative adversarial networks (GANs) \cite{Goodfellow2014GenerativeAN}, have demonstrated the ability to generate realistic data samples that closely mimic the original inputs, further exacerbating privacy concerns. To rigorously assess the implications of gradient-based privacy attacks, it is imperative to formalize the underlying problem. Consider a federated learning system comprising $N$ participants, each with a local dataset $\mathcal{D}_i = \{(\mathbf{x}_i^j, \mathbf{y}_i^j)\}_{j=1}^{m_i}$, where $(\mathbf{x}_i^j, \mathbf{y}_i^j)$ denotes the $j$-th input-label pair in the $i$-th participant's dataset. The global objective is to minimize the aggregate loss function:  egin{equation} \mathcal{L}_{	ext{global}}(\mathbf{W}) = \sum_{i=1}^{N}  rac{m_i}{m} \mathcal{L}_i(\mathbf{W}), \end{equation} where $m = \sum_{i=1}^{N} m_i$ is the total number of data points across all participants, and $\mathcal{L